From 022907ce9753bf65e0efc450f3f06f52400eba84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 03:02:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): classify chat-link and backfill sessions in
 batches, not per row [daveey]

Hub sync loop rounds slowed from ~1 min to 15-28 min (t_01a1d2cc49634a78): the
replica pull, outbox push and remote-parent mirror waited behind the links pass.
A 60 s py-spy --idle profile of the sync thread put 65% of its wall time in
_local_session_sources; 60% came from _owner_written_releases' per-row
is_machine_session. _local_links (created loop, _worker_shepherd), reconcile's
placeholder check, _machine_releases and creator_chats.backfill did the same.
Backfill did it inside write_txn, holding the board write lock while the
dispatcher's pull waited.

Each per-row call opened every home's state.db once its 300 s cache entry was
missing or expired, so a slow pass outlived the cache and fed on itself.

- provenance: headless_sessions() (batched is_headless_session) and
  prefetch_sources() (one read per home for a pass's refs).
- chat links: every verdict in a pass is classified up front per exact chat
  profile; outputs unchanged.
- backfill: headless check and a chat-link prefetch run before write_txn.

Tests: 500 sessions x 3 homes now cost 3 state.db opens (base: 337 in
_local_links, 1206 when the pass outlives the TTL, 834 in
_owner_written_releases, 1467 inside backfill's write_txn); byte-identical
output against the per-row implementations kept verbatim as oracles.
